ink it's all going to be predictable  just as it was again  this happens take us through the process  from now and i mean is there a time  frame on what we can expect  well yes the israeli president has  a certain period during which to assess  who is the most likely  of the contenders to be able to form a  coalition government that is usually the  leader  of the party the single party that has  got got the most votes in the election  so  that is likely to be benjamin netanyahu  and then he would have  in the first instance four weeks from  that point  to try to put together such a coalition  but this  development uh which looked uh  not to have happened when the exit polls  and even hours later after the exit  polls came out  or late on tuesday night with  the united arab list having not  apparently made it past a 3.25 percent  share of the vote required even to get  in  to the parliament now that they have  done it has really  uh set everything uh on its head because  now netanyahu's block is  under the margin so is the potential  opposition  there are those in liukud who are  talking about potentially working with  mansoor abbas the leader of this party  who has  tried to position uh the party since  deciding to break away from the rest  of the joint list as uh somewhere in the  middle between those those two rival  camps between sort of the mainstream and  even right-wing  zionist parties and the the  palestinian-israeli  arab parties he says he's doing so for  pragmatic reasons to try to  get policy on the ground in favor of his  constituents  and that to do such a thing he would  consider working  with either the right or the left camp  he doesn't want a label  but if he worked with netanyahu and  there are two ways he could do that  either  unprecedentedly entering a coalition  with the liquid and its other right wing  allies  or by promising not to vote against a  minority government  uh made up of those constituent parts if  that happened he would find himself  working on the same side  as this extreme right-wing grouping the  religious zionist movement which  contains  descendants of a party that was deemed  so anti-arab racist  in the 1990s that it was banned from the  knesset all together so huge  contradictions there  but also on the other side too there is  a broad ideological spectrum  of anti-netanyahu parties some of whom  are themselves  very right-wing even further right-wing  than netanyahu who also say they would  not work with what they see as a  conservative islamist  party such as that led by mansour abbas  so it's extraordinarily  complicated the gears are grinding once  again  in this long-running political crisis  and  still no obvious resolution at hand  fascinating isn't it  so interesting thank you harry fawcett  in west jerusalem  a little more now on what it takes and  what's in play to form the government in  israel it's the knesset it's a 120  member legislator  just so the magic number to come to  power the ones in blue 61 of them now  projections show that that number  all of those blue ones would elude  netanyahu and his likud party  also likely not possible for the  coalition of parties which oppose them  and as we were just hearing from harry  that small palestinian israeli party  which the pulses say could win as many  as five seats  could be holding uh the balance of power  israel's electoral system it's a complex  form of proportional representation  which  as we've seen makes it quite difficult  for any single party to win  an election outright more with our  senior political analyst now  marwan bashara marwan i don't even think  we mentioned the name  in mansur yesterday uh when we were  going through  all the possibilities and what could  happen suddenly  he sees himself in the spotlight and as  harry was saying possibly not just with  likud but with the far  right party even for israeli politics  this is pretty extraordinary  it is uh extraordinary that's why i  don't think it's realistic  um you know theoretically anything can  happen  theoretically there is no difference  between theoretically and practically  in practice there is so  i don't think in real life we're going  to see  uh naftali bennett of yamina party  the extreme right-wing party sit with  abbas in the same coalition  certainly won't say the zionist  religious party  the most extreme of most extreme  fundamentalist  jewish parties sitting together in the  same coalition  as an islamist party i don't see it  at all what i will see is in the next  month  or 45 days that there will be a lot of  maneuvering and netanyahu will use abbas  and abbas will use netanyahu in order  for both of them  to try to entice other parties to join  but those with a bit of a memory  kamal will remember how netanyahu  in the mid 90s incited against  then prime minister itzhak rabin  for daring to depend  on a palestinian party  in the knesset in order to pass  a a resolution uh within parliament  so much so that that incitement was  whipped up  so feverishly that it led to the  assassination  of yitzhak rabin in 1995. that was  netanyahu and that  is still netanyahu netanyahu that  incited to the killing of  one israeli prime minister because he  depended  on and on an arab party will not  depend on an arab party himself  it's 2021 though now marwan and i  i feel i wonder is netanyahu more  desperate now to hold on to power given  everything he's gone through given he's  been the prime minister for so long the  corruption charges and all these things  never know what he might do  this is what's so both fascinating and  disturbing  about this whole episode because we are  now faced with a situation that  basically the one person who can  theoretically form a government  would be netanyahu and i think he would  be doing it by peeling off  some of the members of the of the of the  mid-sized parties  offering them ministerial positions or  even mid-sized parties themselves like  blue white party or whoever  and and offering them really enticing  offers in order for them to join the  government  and even that is a bit far-fetched  because he  doesn't only want him to join the  government is to change  the judiciary both in order to please  the religious parties and in order for  him not to go on trial so i think that  would be his path  however and i think this is probably the  thing that we have not discussed yet  that while he could be the deal maker  that he  is the deal maker he is also the biggest  obstacle for the formation  of a liquid-led coalition  government if netanyahu  leaves the scene or nudged off  the helm of the liquid party somehow  likud then could form a coalition  government by a good number  of mid-sized parties in israel  including its nemesis yashatid including  blue and white party including the new  whole party  including yamina party and so on so  forth  so likud has potential to form a  government it wouldn't be great for  israel  it won't be great for the palestinians  it won't be great for peace  but it is possible if netanyahu leaves  the scene  wow it's going to be interesting isn't  it thank you marwan bashara is our  senior political analyst  thank you

